L2 Knowledge Base: Floor 6 Legal Slow/Failed Windows Logon (Win11 + Intune)
v 1.0, 07/08/2026, status : Draft

Purpose
Provide a repeatable L2 procedure for floor-wide slow/failed Windows sign-in affecting the recent Win11 + Intune migrated cohort.

Scope and trigger
- Use this article when multiple users on the same floor report slow/failed sign-in.
- Do not use this article for isolated single-user desktop issues.

Required access
- Intune admin rights to view/edit policy assignments and trigger device sync.
- Entra ID sign-in log access.
- Local admin on at least one affected endpoint for event log collection.
- Incident and change record access.

Inputs to collect first
- Incident ticket ID
- One affected user ID
- One affected host name
- One control user/host pair (if available)
- Recent Win11/Intune change ticket ID for affected floor

Technical procedure with navigation paths

Step 1: Set ownership and baseline in ticket
- Navigation path: Service Desk tool > Incident > Open active ticket > Status
- Action: Set ticket status to In Progress - Login Outage Triage.
- Expected result: Ownership and start timestamp are recorded.

Step 2: Record fixed test pair
- Navigation path: Service Desk tool > Incident > Work notes
- Action: Record affected user ID and affected host name.
- Expected result: Repeatable validation pair is established.

Step 3: Collect device join state
- Navigation path: Remote support session > PowerShell 5.1 (Run as admin)
- Action: Run `dsregcmd /status` and paste output to ticket.
- Expected result: Join/token state captured for correlation.

Step 4: Export authentication events
- Navigation path: Endpoint > Event Viewer > Windows Logs > Security > Filter Current Log
- Action: Filter/export last 2 hours for IDs 4624, 4625, 4771, 4776.
- Expected result: Authentication success/failure evidence available.

Step 5: Export profile service events
- Navigation path: Endpoint > Event Viewer > Applications and Services Logs > Microsoft > Windows > User Profiles Service > Operational
- Action: Export last 2 hours of events.
- Expected result: Profile load/failure evidence captured.

Step 6: Trigger first device sync
- Navigation path: Intune admin center > Devices > All devices > <affected device> > Sync
- Action: Run manual Sync on affected device.
- Expected result: Fresh policy check-in initiated.

Step 7: Capture current policy assignment
- Navigation path: Intune admin center > Devices > Configuration profiles > <suspected sign-in/profile policy> > Assignments
- Action: Screenshot/export current assignment scope.
- Expected result: Pre-change assignment evidence preserved.

Step 8: Contain by scope-limited policy pause/unassign
- Navigation path: Intune admin center > Devices > Configuration profiles > <suspected policy> > Assignments
- Action: Pause or unassign policy from Floor 6 device group only.
- Expected result: Suspected setting stops applying to Floor 6.

Step 9: Trigger second device sync
- Navigation path: Intune admin center > Devices > All devices > <affected device> > Sync
- Action: Run a second manual Sync.
- Expected result: Updated effective policy requested by endpoint.

Step 10: Re-test affected user sign-in
- Navigation path: Endpoint sign-in screen > User credentials submit
- Action: Ask user to sign in on same host and time credential submit to desktop-ready.
- Expected result: User reaches desktop within expected baseline.

Step 11: Run control validation
- Navigation path: Control host > Sign-in
- Action: Test one additional user on one additional Floor 6 host.
- Expected result: Confirms service recovery is not single-device only.

Step 12: Confirm sync completion
- Navigation path: Intune admin center > Devices > All devices > <tested devices> > Device status
- Action: Verify sync completion for both tested hosts.
- Expected result: Post-containment policy refresh is complete.

Step 13: Record restoration evidence and handoff
- Navigation path: Service Desk tool > Incident > Work notes and resolution
- Action: Attach logs, sync timestamps, sign-in timings, and mark Service Restored.
- Expected result: Auditable closure evidence is complete.

Verification before close
- Affected user can sign in to original affected host.
- Sign-in duration is back to floor baseline.
- Additional user on additional host signs in successfully.
- No repeating auth/profile failure pattern appears for 30 minutes.
- Intune shows successful sync on tested hosts.

Rollback if behavior worsens after containment

Rollback 1
- Navigation path: Intune admin center > Devices > Configuration profiles > <suspected policy> > Assignments
- Action: Restore policy assignment exactly to pre-change scope captured in Step 7.
- Expected result: Original targeting reinstated.

Rollback 2
- Navigation path: Intune admin center > Devices > All devices > <test devices> > Sync
- Action: Trigger Sync on each test device.
- Expected result: Restored assignment requested by endpoints.

Rollback 3
- Navigation path: Endpoint > Start > Power > Restart
- Action: Reboot each test host once.
- Expected result: Hosts reload policy state.

Rollback 4
- Navigation path: Endpoint sign-in screen
- Action: Re-test sign-in with original affected user.
- Expected result: Post-rollback behavior confirmed and documented.

Rollback 5
- Navigation path: Service Desk tool > Incident > Escalate
- Action: Escalate to L2 endpoint engineering with logs and assignment diff.
- Expected result: Complete technical context transferred for takeover.

Operational warnings
- Do not roll back policy outside Floor 6 scope during containment.
- Do not delete user profiles during active hours without explicit approval.
- Do not close incident from a single successful sign-in test.

